Base62 uses `[A-Za-z0-9]` — 62 symbols, all URL-safe without escaping, all human-typeable. Compare:

- **Base16** (hex) — only 16 symbols → codes are 1.7× longer.
- **Base64** (URL-safe) — 64 symbols but includes `-` and `_`, which look like punctuation in URLs and confuse users.
- **Base62** — sweet spot. 7 chars = 3.5 trillion codes.

The encode loop is trivial:

```java
private static final String ALPHABET =
    "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789";

public static String encodeBase62(long id) {
    if (id == 0) return "A";
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    while (id > 0) {
        sb.append(ALPHABET.charAt((int) (id % 62)));
        id /= 62;
    }
    return sb.reverse().toString();
}
```

At our scale, the 41-bit timestamp portion of a Snowflake ID dominates the encoded length. After ~5 years, codes hit 8 characters; we either accept that or reset the epoch.

Layered, with each role behind an interface so we can swap implementations.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class ShortenController {
        +POST createShortUrl(req)
        +GET getStats(code)
    }
    class RedirectController {
        +GET resolve(code)
    }
    class ShortenService {
        +shorten(longUrl, alias) Url
    }
    class RedirectService {
        +resolve(code) String
    }
    class IdGenerator {
        <<interface>>
        +nextId() long
    }
    class SnowflakeIdGenerator {
        +nextId() long
    }
    class UrlRepository {
        <<interface>>
        +save(url) Url
        +findByCode(code) Optional~Url~
    }
    class JdbcUrlRepository
    class UrlCache {
        +get(code) Optional~String~
        +put(code, longUrl, ttl)
    }
    class ClickEventPublisher {
        +publish(event)
    }

    ShortenController --> ShortenService
    RedirectController --> RedirectService
    ShortenService --> IdGenerator
    ShortenService --> UrlRepository
    ShortenService --> UrlCache
    RedirectService --> UrlCache
    RedirectService --> UrlRepository
    RedirectService --> ClickEventPublisher
    IdGenerator <|.. SnowflakeIdGenerator
    UrlRepository <|.. JdbcUrlRepository
```

Key method shapes (only the interesting bodies):

```java
public class ShortenService {
    public Url shorten(String longUrl, @Nullable String customAlias, @Nullable Instant expiresAt) {
        validateUrl(longUrl);                                 // scheme, length, blocklist

        String code = (customAlias != null)
            ? reserveAlias(customAlias)                       // INSERT ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING
            : Base62.encode(idGenerator.nextId());

        Url url = new Url(code, longUrl, currentUserId(), expiresAt);
        repo.save(url);                                       // primary DB write
        cache.put(code, longUrl, Duration.ofHours(24));       // warm hot path
        return url;
    }
}

public class RedirectService {
    public String resolve(String code) {
        Optional<String> cached = cache.get(code);
        if (cached.isPresent()) {
            publisher.publish(new ClickEvent(code, Instant.now()));   // async
            return cached.get();
        }
        Url url = repo.findByCode(code).orElseThrow(NotFoundException::new);
        if (url.isExpired()) throw new GoneException();
        cache.put(code, url.longUrl(), Duration.ofHours(24));
        publisher.publish(new ClickEvent(code, Instant.now()));
        return url.longUrl();
    }
}
```

Why this shape:

- **`IdGenerator` is an interface** — in tests we use a deterministic counter; in prod, Snowflake.
- **`UrlRepository` is interface-first** — swap JDBC for DynamoDB without touching the service.
- **`ClickEventPublisher` is fire-and-forget** — the redirect never blocks on Kafka; the publisher buffers and flushes.
- **Cache write on shorten** warms the hot path before any user clicks the link, eliminating cold-start misses for popular launches.
